{
  "id": "roman-roads",
  "cat": "People",
  "title": "All roads lead to Rome",
  "hook": "The Romans built about 300,000 km of roads, from Scotland to the Nile. A new atlas maps every one of them, and shows how few we can trace for certain.",
  "notes": "Roads: Itiner-e static version 2024 (de Soto, Pažout, Brughmans et al., Zenodo 17122148, v1.3, CC BY 4.0 as stated at https://itiner-e.org/about and in the published Zenodo record rights; described in Scientific Data, 2025, doi:10.1038/s41597-025-06140-z). It covers the roads of the Roman Empire around 300 BC to AD 300 in 14,769 sections, drawn from atlases, excavation and survey reports and milestones. The network was never complete at any one moment: roads were built, rebuilt and abandoned over six centuries, so the map shows everything known, not one year. Lengths are the dataset's own; Orbis simplified the lines for display (tolerance about 2 km for main roads, 5 km for secondary roads). Certainty is Itiner-e's grade for how precisely the course is known: certain (within 50 m in mountains, 200 m in plains), conjectured (the road is known, its exact course less so) and hypothetical (not localised, desert tracks, or speculated). Named roads are the sections Itiner-e tags with that name; their length is the sum of those sections, which may not cover the whole ancient road. Building dates on the cards come from Itiner-e's construction field (the magistrate or emperor under whom a road was built), which is filled for about a fifth of the network; the Via Aemilia's entry reads '189–187 CE', a slip for BC (Livy 39.2 dates it to 187 BC). Distances in the reach scene are straight lines between the end points of the outermost sections. City locations: Pleiades gazetteer of ancient places (CC BY 3.0).",
